arm64: x86 memory ordering per thread on Apple silicon (PR_SET_MEM_MODEL)

Apple silicon can run with x86's memory ordering, total store ordering, per
thread: bit 1 of the implementation-defined ACTLR_EL1, advertised by bit 9
of AIDR_EL1. Rosetta-translated code assumes that ordering, and Rosetta asks
for it itself, twice at startup, with prctl(0x4d4d444c, 1); a kernel that
answers EINVAL leaves its threads on ARM's model, and multi-threaded x86
programs then see stores out of order. The alternative, the hypervisor
switching every vCPU into TSO for the whole machine, costs native code:
memcpy bandwidth fell by a quarter (45-48 GB/s to 31-38) with nothing else
moving, measured 2026-09-05.

So the model is a thread's, kept in thread_struct, inherited across fork and
clone, reset by exec, and written to the register only when the incoming
thread's setting differs from what this CPU last had. The register's other
bits are preserved from the boot value. Whether the bit can be written at
all is proved at boot rather than assumed, because a hypervisor may present
it and ignore it, and x86 code would then run with the wrong ordering
silently. The prctl numbers and semantics follow the series Hector Martin
sent upstream in April 2024 and Apple's own patch of the same month, which
is what Rosetta was written against.

